          "path": "_m/d/h/q/x/everglades-national-park-wp/american-crocodile-survey-5443.jpg",
          "filename": "American Crocodile Survey (54436658471).jpg",
          "caption": "These images highlight the work behind American crocodile nest surveys in Everglades National Park’s Flamingo District. From early morning field checks to nest flagging and habitat documentation, each photo reflects the intersection of science and conservation in action.\nNesting sites appear in unexpected places along roads, near marinas, and amidst human activity. As rising seas and shifting landscapes challenge traditional nesting grounds, crocodiles are adapting. And so are we. Park teams monitor each site closely to protect eggs and hatchlings from unintentional impacts, such as roadside maintenance or foot traffic.\nEvery flagged mound, field note, and snapshot in this collection supports a larger mission: preserving biodiversity and helping a threatened species coexist in a world shaped by change.\n\nNPS Photo by Brandon Cintron Gerena",
